n\"><a href=\"#S6.SS5\" title=\"In 6 How to Model an Audio Latent ‣ A Unifying Perspective on Audio Generative Modeling: Latent Representations and Modeling Strategies\" class=\"ltx_ref\"><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_ref_title\"><span class=\"ltx_tag ltx_tag_ref\">6.5 </span>What should a unified audio model share?</span></a></li>\n223\t</ol>\n224\t</li>\n225\t<li class=\"ltx_tocentry ltx_tocentry_section\">\n226\t<a href=\"#S7\" title=\"In A Unifying Perspective on Audio Generative Modeling: Latent Representations and Modeling Strategies\" class=\"ltx_ref\"><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_ref_title\"><span class=\"ltx_tag ltx_tag_ref\">7 </span>Open Problems in Audio Generative Modeling</span></a>\n227\t<ol class=\"ltx_toclist ltx_toclist_section\">\n228\t<li class=\"ltx_tocentry ltx_tocentry_subsection\"><a href=\"#S7.SS1\" title=\"In 7 Open Problems in Audio Generative Modeling ‣ A Unifying Perspective on Audio Generative Modeling: Latent Representations and Modeling Strategies\" class=\"ltx_ref\"><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_ref_title\"><span class=\"ltx_tag ltx_tag_ref\">7.1 </span>Evaluate modelability under controlled conditions</span></a></li>\n229\t<li class=\"ltx_tocentry ltx_tocentry_subsection\"><a href=\"#S7.SS2\" title=\"In 7 Open Problems in Audio Generative Modeling ‣ A Unifying Perspective on Audio Generative Modeling: Latent Representations and Modeling Strategies\" class=\"ltx_ref\"><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_ref_title\"><span class=\"ltx_tag ltx_tag_ref\">7.2 </span>Co-design the latent and its modeling strategy</span></a></li>\n230\t<li class=\"ltx_tocentry ltx_tocentry_subsection\"><a href=\"#S7.SS3\" title=\"In 7 Open Problems in Audio Generative Modeling ‣ A Unifying Perspective on Audio Generative Modeling: Latent Representations and Modeling Strategies\" class=\"ltx_ref\"><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_ref_title\"><span class=\"ltx_tag ltx_tag_ref\">7.3 </span>Design the latent and model jointly for streaming</span></a></li>\n231\t<li class=\"ltx_tocentry ltx_tocentry_subsection\"><a href=\"#S7.SS4\" title=\"In 7 Open Problems in Audio Generative Modeling ‣ A Unifying Perspective on Audio Generative Modeling: Latent Representations and Modeling Strategies\" class=\"ltx_ref\"><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_ref_title\"><span class=\"ltx_tag ltx_tag_ref\">7.4 </span>Decide what requires long-context modeling</span></a></li>\n232\t</ol>\n233\t</li>\n234\t<li class=\"ltx_tocentry ltx_tocentry_section\"><a href=\"#S8\" title=\"In A Unifying Perspective on Audio Generative Modeling: Latent Representations and Modeling Strategies\" class=\"ltx_ref\"><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_ref_title\"><span class=\"ltx_tag ltx_tag_ref\">8 </span>Conclusion</span></a></li>\n235\t<li class=\"ltx_tocentry ltx_tocentry_bibliography\"><a href=\"#bib\" title=\"In A Unifying Perspective on Audio Generative Modeling: Latent Representations and Modeling Strategies\" class=\"ltx_ref\"><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_ref_title\">References</span></a></li>\n236\t<li class=\"ltx_tocentry ltx_tocentry_appendix\">\n237\t<a href=\"#A1\" title=\"In A Unifying Perspective on Audio Generative Modeling: Latent Representations and Modeling Strategies\" class=\"ltx_ref\"><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_ref_title\"><span class=\"ltx_tag ltx_tag_ref\">A </span>Why Audio Needs a Latent</span></a>\n238\t<ol class=\"ltx_toclist ltx_toclist_appendix\">\n239\t<li class=\"ltx_tocentry ltx_tocentry_subsection\"><a href=\"#A1.SS1\" title=\"In Appendix A Why Audio Needs a Latent ‣ A Unifying Perspective on Audio Generative Modeling: Latent Representations and Modeling Strategies\" class=\"ltx_ref\"><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_ref_title\"><span class=\"ltx_tag ltx_tag_ref\">A.1 </span>Mel as the first widely used latent</span></a></li>\n240\t<li class=\"ltx_tocentry ltx_tocentry_subsection\"><a href=\"#A1.SS2\" title=\"In Appendix A Why Audio Needs a Latent ‣ A Unifying Perspective on Audio Generative Modeling: Latent Representations and Modeling Strategies\" class=\"ltx_ref\"><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_ref_title\"><span class=\"ltx_tag ltx_tag_ref\">A.2 </span>What may be discarded, and what must remain</span></a></li>\n241\t</ol>\n242\t</li>\n243\t<li class=\"ltx_tocentry ltx_tocentry_appendix\">\n244\t<a href=\"#A2\" title=\"In A Unifying Perspective on Audio Generative Modeling: Latent Representations and Modeling Strategies\" class=\"ltx_ref\"><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_ref_title\"><span class=\"ltx_tag ltx_tag_ref\">B </span>From VQ-VAE to Modern Audio Codecs</span></a>\n245\t<ol class=\"ltx_toclist ltx_toclist_appendix\">\n246\t<li class=\"ltx_tocentry ltx_tocentry_subsection\"><a href=\"#A2.SS1\" title=\"In Appendix B From VQ-VAE to Modern Audio Codecs ‣ A Unifying Perspective on Audio Generative Modeling: Latent Representations and Modeling Strategies\" class=\"ltx_ref\"><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_ref_title\"><span class=\"ltx_tag ltx_tag_ref\">B.1 </span>A reconstructable token stream may still be difficult to model</span></a></li>\n247\t</ol>\n248\t</li>\n249\t<li class=\"ltx_tocentry ltx_tocentry_appendix\">\n250\t<a href=\"#A3\" title=\"In A Unifying Perspective on Audio Generative Modeling: Latent Representations and Modeling Strategies\" class=\"ltx_ref\"><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_ref_title\"><span class=\"ltx_tag ltx_tag_ref\">C </span>How Audio Models Consume Codec Tokens</span></a>\n251\t<ol class=\"ltx_toclist ltx_toclist_appendix\">\n252\t<li class=\"ltx_tocentry ltx_tocentry_subsection\"><a href=\"#A3.SS1\" title=\"In Appendix C How Audio Models Consume Codec Tokens ‣ A Unifying Perspective on Audio Generative Modeling: Latent Representations and Modeling Strategies\" class=\"ltx_ref\"><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_ref_title\"><span class=\"ltx_tag ltx_tag_ref\">C.1 </span>Two axes, several valid factorizations</span></a></li>\n253\t</ol>\n254\t</li>\n255\t<li class=\"ltx_tocentry ltx_tocentry_appendix\"><a href=\"#A4\" title=\"In A Unifying Perspective on Audio Generative Modeling: Latent Representations and Modeling Strategies\" class=\"ltx_ref\"><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_ref_title\"><span class=\"ltx_tag ltx_tag_ref\">D </span>The Fixed-Rate Grid and Content-Adaptive Latents</span></a></li>\n256\t</ol></nav>\n257\t</nav>\n258\t<div class=\"ltx_page_main\">\n259\t<div id=\"infobox\" class=\"infobox\">\n260\t  <a id=\"license-tr\" href=\"https://info.arxiv.org/help/license/index.html#licenses-available\">\n261\t    License: arXiv.org perpetual non-exclusive license\n262\t  </a>\n263\t  <div id=\"watermark-tr\">\n264\tarXiv:2608.08667v1 [eess.AS] 09 Aug 2026</div>\n265\t</div><div class=\"ltx_page_content\">\n266\t<article class=\"ltx_document ltx_authors_1line\">\n267\t<h1 class=\"ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_document\">A Unifying Perspective on Audio Generative Modeling:\n268\t<br class=\"ltx_break\"> Latent Representations and Modeling Strategies</h1>\n269\t<div class=\"ltx_authors\">\n270\t<span class=\"ltx_creator ltx_role_author\">\n271\t<span class=\"ltx_personname\">Dongchao Yang\n272\t<br class=\"ltx_break\">The Chinese University of Hong Kong\n273\t<br class=\"ltx_break\"><span id=\"id1.1.id1\" class=\"ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter\">dcyang@se.cuhk.edu.hk</span>\n274\t</span><span class=\"ltx_author_notes\">This is a preliminary version. Comments and corrections are welcome.</span></span>\n275\t</div>\n276\t\n277\t<div id=\"abstract1\" class=\"ltx_abstract\">\n278\t<h6 class=\"ltx_title ltx_title_abstract\">Abstract</h6>\n279\t<p class=\"ltx_p\">Every audio generative system makes two coupled decisions: what representation to generate, and how to model its distribution. This paper organizes audio generative modeling around this coupling. For representation design, we compare discrete, continuous, and hybrid latents through four objectives: representation burden, distortion, empirical modelability, and streaming compatibility. For distribution modeling, rather than treating a latent’s difficulty as an intrinsic scalar, we use two diagnostic dimensions: dependency horizon, how far useful context extends, and conditional ambiguity, how much uncertainty remains after conditioning. These dimensions refine the common semantic-versus-acoustic intuition: variables with a long dependency horizon should receive global modeling capacity. Conditionally ambiguous detail may be delegated to a local or iterative generator. Applied to representative systems, this view shows that RVQ’s residual order gives ordered capacity but not ordered semantics, that AudioLM’s semantic-versus-acoustic cascade is one explicit placement of this boundary rather than a universal template, and that autoregression, iterative refinement, and hybrid designs differ chiefly in how they trade dependency horizon against critical-path generation cost. The distinction between discrete and continuous latents describes the output interface; dependency horizon, conditional ambiguity, and streaming determine how that interface should be modeled. Rather than cataloguing individual systems, we provide an evaluation and design framework for comparing representation–model pairs.</p>\n280\t</div>\n281\t<section id=\"S1\" class=\"ltx_section\">\n282\t<h2 class=\"ltx_title ltx_title_section\">\n283\t<span class=\"ltx_tag ltx_tag_section\">1 </span>Introduction</h2>\n284\t\n285\t<div id=\"S1.p1\" class=\"ltx_para\">\n286\t<p id=\"S1.p1.1\" class=\"ltx_p\">Every audio generative system makes two coupled choices: what latent representation to generate, and how to model its distribution. Although these choices are often implemented in separate modules, with an encoder or codec followed by a generative model, they cannot be evaluated independently. The latent determines the sequence length, the information carried at each step, and the dependencies exposed to the generator. The modeling strategy determines how those dependencies are factorized and how the remaining uncertainty is represented. Together, they determine generation quality, computational cost, and whether the system can operate in real time. We aim to provide a structured perspective on audio generative modeling, not an exhaustive catalogue or a theorem about one optimal architecture.</p>\n287\t</div>\n288\t<div id=\"S1.p2\" class=\"ltx_para\">\n289\t<p id=\"S1.p2.1\" class=\"ltx_p\">SoundStream <cite class=\"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep\">[<a href=\"#bib.bib1\" title=\"\" class=\"ltx_ref\"><span class=\"ltx_text\" style=\"font-size:90%;\">1</span></a>]</cite> and EnCodec <cite class=\"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep\">[<a href=\"#bib.bib2\" title=\"\" class=\"ltx_ref\"><span class=\"ltx_text\" style=\"font-size:90%;\">2</span></a>]</cite> illustrate one form of this coupling. They keep the temporal frame rate low by representing each frame with multiple codebook indices through residual vector quantization (RVQ). The resulting latent has two dependency axes, time and codebook depth, and is no longer a standard one-token-per-step LM target. Flattening both axes preserves rich conditioning but increases serial generation cost. Predicting codebooks in parallel is faster but removes part of their conditional structure. VALL-E <cite class=\"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep\">[<a href=\"#bib.bib3\" title=\"\" class=\"ltx_ref\"><span class=\"ltx_text\" style=\"font-size:90%;\">3</span></a>]</cite>, MusicGen <cite class=\"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep\">[<a href=\"#bib.bib4\" title=\"\" class=\"ltx_ref\"><span class=\"ltx_text\" style=\"font-size:90%;\">4</span></a>]</cite>, UniAudio <cite class=\"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep\">[<a href=\"#bib.bib5\" title=\"\" class=\"ltx_ref\"><span class=\"ltx_text\" style=\"font-size:90%;\">5</span></a>]</cite>, and Moshi <cite class=\"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep\">[<a href=\"#bib.bib6\" title=\"\" class=\"ltx_ref\"><span class=\"ltx_text\" style=\"font-size:90%;\">6</span></a>]</cite> choose different compromises between these two costs. The codec has therefore not only compressed the waveform; it has also determined the factorization problem faced by the downstream model. AudioLM <cite class=\"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep\">[<a href=\"#bib.bib7\" title=\"\" class=\"ltx_ref\"><span class=\"ltx_text\" style=\"font-size:90%;\">7</span></a>]</cite> illustrates a second form of the same coupling: it shows where a representation draws the boundary between global content and local, ambiguous detail. Semantic tokens make long-range linguistic structure directly predictable but do not preserve enough information for waveform reconstruction. We interpret AudioLM as an explicit allocation: it predicts a semantic trajectory first and generates acoustic tokens conditionally, drawing this boundary between two separate token vocabularies. DiTAR <cite class=\"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep\">[<a href=\"#bib.bib8\" title=\"\" class=\"ltx_ref\"><span class=\"ltx_text\" style=\"font-size:90%;\">8</span></a>]</cite> and CALM <cite class=\"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep\">[<a href=\"#bib.bib9\" title=\"\" class=\"ltx_ref\"><span class=\"ltx_text\" style=\"font-size:90%;\">9</span></a>]</cite> draw it inside a single continuous latent instead. A causal outer process tracks history. Diffusion or a consistency head resolves the remaining acoustic ambiguity within each step or block.</p>\n290\t</div>\n291\t<div id=\"S1.p3\" class=\"ltx_para\">\n292\t<p id=\"S1.p3.1\" class=\"ltx_p\">RVQ’s codebook depth and a continuous latent’s per-token dimensionality buy the same representation burden in two different ways. AudioLM’s semantic tokenizer and DiTAR’s and CALM’s causal-outer, diffusion-inner split draw the same global-versus-local boundary in two different ways. Discreteness and continuity decide neither outcome. What varies across these systems is how dependency structure and conditional ambiguity are allocated, not the representation’s category.</p>\n293\t</div>\n294\t<div id=\"S1.p4\" class=\"ltx_para\">\n295\t<p id=\"S1.p4.1\" class=\"ltx_p\">These examples motivate the two questions that organize the paper. <span id=\"S1.p4.1.1\" class=\"ltx_text ltx_font_bold\">What should an audio model generate?</span> We compare discrete, continuous, and hybrid latents through four requirements: rate, distortion, modelability, and streaming. <span id=\"S1.p4.1.2\" class=\"ltx_text ltx_font_bold\">How should the distribution of that latent be modeled?</span> We examine how far its dependencies extend, how much ambiguity remains after conditioning, and whether future context is available. These properties determine when long-context autoregression, iterative refinement, codebook-depth modeling, or a hybrid factorization is appropriate.</p>\n296\t</div>\n297\t<div id=\"S1.p5\" class=\"ltx_para\">\n298\t<p id=\"S1.p5.1\" class=\"ltx_p\">The analysis proceeds in three steps. We first describe a four-objective representation design space. We then refine the intuition that semantics is low-entropy and acoustics is high-entropy, using two diagnostic questions: which variables benefit from longer context, and which retain multiple acceptable realizations after conditioning? Finally, we analyze how different modeling schemes assign these variables to long-context, local, or iterative models. Appendix <a href=\"#A1\" title=\"Appendix A Why Audio Needs a Latent ‣ A Unifying Perspective on Audio Generative Modeling: Latent Representations and Modeling Strategies\" class=\"ltx_ref\"><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_ref_tag\">A</span></a> provides the codec lineage and technical background omitted from the main argument.</p>\n299\t</div>\n300\t<section id=\"S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1\" class=\"ltx_paragraph\">\n301\t<h4 class=\"ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph\">Contributions.</h4>\n302\t\n303\t<div id=\"S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1\" class=\"ltx_para\">\n304\t<ul id=\"S1.I1\" class=\"ltx_itemize\">\n305\t<li id=\"S1.I1.i1\" class=\"ltx_item\" style=\"list-style-type:none;\">\n306\t<span class=\"ltx_tag ltx_tag_item\">•</span> \n307\t<div id=\"S1.I1.i1.p1\" class=\"ltx_para\">\n308\t<p id=\"S1.I1.i1.p1.1\" class=\"ltx_p\">We organize audio generative modeling around the coupled design of an audio latent and the model that learns its distribution.</p>\n309\t</div>\n310\t</li>\n311\t<li id=\"S1.I1.i2\" class=\"ltx_item\" style=\"list-style-type:none;\">\n312\t<span class=\"ltx_tag ltx_tag_item\">•</span> \n313\t<div id=\"S1.I1.i2.p1\" class=\"ltx_para\">\n314\t<p id=\"S1.I1.i2.p1.1\" class=\"ltx_p\">We formulate representation design as a four-objective Pareto problem over burden, distortion, empirical modelability, and streaming compatibility.</p>\n315\t</div>\n316\t</li>\n317\t<li id=\"S1.I1.i3\" class=\"ltx_item\" style=\"list-style-type:none;\">\n318\t<span class=\"ltx_tag ltx_tag_item\">•</span> \n319\t<div id=\"S1.I1.i3.p1\" class=\"ltx_para\">\n320\t<p id=\"S1.I1.i3.p1.1\" class=\"ltx_p\">We use dependency horizon, conditional ambiguity, approximation sources, and critical-path evaluations as diagnostic tools for comparing autoregressive, codebook-depth, iterative, and hybrid systems, and apply them to representative systems.</p>\n321\t</div>\n322\t</li>\n323\t</ul>\n324\t</div>\n325\t</section>\n326\t</section>\n327\t<section id=\"S2\" class=\"ltx_section\">\n328\t<h2 class=\"ltx_title ltx_title_section\">\n329\t<span class=\"ltx_tag ltx_tag_section\">2 </span>Two Decisions Behind Audio Generation</h2>\n330\t\n331\t<div id=\"S2.p1\" class=\"ltx_para\">\n332\t<p id=\"S2.p1.11\" class=\"ltx_p\">Let <math id=\"S2.p1.1.m1\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"x\\in\\mathbb{R}^{L}\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>L</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x\\in\\mathbb{R}^{L}</annotation></semantics></math> denote a waveform of duration <math id=\"S2.p1.2.m2\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"\\mathcal{T}\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mi class=\"ltx_font_mathcaligraphic\">𝒯</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathcal{T}</annotation></semantics></math>. An encoder maps it to <math id=\"S2.p1.3.m3\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"z=(z_{1},\\dots,z_{N})\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z=(z_{1},\\dots,z_{N})</annotation></semantics></math> at frame rate <math id=\"S2.p1.4.m4\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"r=N/\\mathcal{T}\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>/</mo><mi class=\"ltx_font_mathcaligraphic\">𝒯</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r=N/\\mathcal{T}</annotation></semantics></math>. For a discrete latent, each <math id=\"S2.p1.5.m5\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"z_{t}\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> contains one or more codebook indices; for a continuous latent, <math id=\"S2.p1.6.m6\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"z_{t}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d}\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z_{t}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>. We write the encoder and decoder as <math id=\"S2.p1.7.m7\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"f_{\\theta}\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f_{\\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id=\"S2.p1.8.m8\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"g_{\\phi}\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g_{\\phi}</annotation></semantics></math>, and let <math id=\"S2.p1.9.m9\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"p_{\\psi}\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>ψ</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p_{\\psi}</annotation></semantics></math> model <math id=\"S2.p1.10.m10\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"p(z)\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p(z)</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id=\"S2.p1.11.m11\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"p(z\\mid c)\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>∣</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p(z\\mid c)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>\n333\t</div>\n334\t<div id=\"S2.p2\" class=\"ltx_para\">\n335\t<p id=\"S2.p2.4\" class=\"ltx_p\">Two representation-level quantities can be written directly:</p>\n336\t<table id=\"A4.EGx1\" class=\"ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table\">\n337\t\n338\t<tbody id=\"S2.E1\"><tr class=\"ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline\">\n339\t<td class=\"ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft\"></td>\n340\t<td class=\"ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell\"><math id=\"S2.E1.m1\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"\\displaystyle R_{\\mathrm{disc}}\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>disc</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\displaystyle R_{\\mathrm{disc}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>\n341\t<td class=\"ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell\"><math id=\"S2.E1.m2\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"\\displaystyle=r\\sum_{k=1}^{K}\\log_{2}|\\mathcal{Z}_{k}|,\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi></mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\"><munderover><mo movablelimits=\"false\">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>K</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><msub><mi class=\"ltx_font_mathcaligraphic\">𝒵</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\displaystyle=r\\sum_{k=1}^{K}\\log_{2}|\\mathcal{Z}_{k}|,</annotation></semantics></math></td>\n342\t<td class=\"ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell\"><math id=\"S2.E1.m3\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"\\displaystyle D\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\displaystyle D</annotation></semantics></math></td>\n343\t<td class=\"ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell\"><math id=\"S2.E1.m4\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"\\displaystyle=\\mathbb{E}_{x}\\,\\rho\\big(x,g_{\\phi}(f_{\\theta}(x))\\big).\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi></mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">​</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize=\"1.200em\" minsize=\"1.200em\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize=\"1.200em\" minsize=\"1.200em\">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace=\"0em\">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\displaystyle=\\mathbb{E}_{x}\\,\\rho\\big(x,g_{\\phi}(f_{\\theta}(x))\\big).</annotation></semantics></math></td>\n344\t<td class=\"ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright\"></td>\n345\t<td rowspan=\"1\" class=\"ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right\"><span class=\"ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right\">(1)</span></td>\n346\t</tr></tbody>\n347\t</table>\n348\t<p id=\"S2.p2.3\" class=\"ltx_p\"><math id=\"S2.p2.1.m1\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"R_{\\mathrm{disc}}\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>disc</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_{\\mathrm{disc}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the nominal bitrate of a <math id=\"S2.p2.2.m2\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"K\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">K</annotation></semantics></math>-codebook discrete representation, and <math id=\"S2.p2.3.m3\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"D\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D</annotation></semantics></math> is perceptual distortion. Rate-distortion theory motivates comparing these two quantities under a source and distortion measure <cite class=\"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep\">[<a href=\"#bib.bib10\" title=\"\" class=\"ltx_ref\"><span class=\"ltx_text\" style=\"font-size:90%;\">10</span></a>]</cite>. A continuous latent has no codec bitrate until a precision or entropy model is specified, so discrete and continuous rates should not be collapsed into one scalar. For discrete latents, we report frame rate, codebook depth, and nominal bitrate; for continuous latents, we report frame rate, dimension, and numerical precision.</p>\n349\t</div>\n350\t<div id=\"S2.p3\" class=\"ltx_para\">\n351\t<p id=\"S2.p3.7\" class=\"ltx_p\">Modelability is not a third intrinsic scalar. We report it as an empirical profile indexed by task <math id=\"S2.p3.1.m1\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"q\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q</annotation></semantics></math>, dataset <math id=\"S2.p3.2.m2\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"\\mathcal{D}\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mi class=\"ltx_font_mathcaligraphic\">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathcal{D}</annotation></semantics></math>, conditioning <math id=\"S2.p3.3.m3\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"c\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">c</annotation></semantics></math>, modeling scheme <math id=\"S2.p3.4.m4\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"G\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">G</annotation></semantics></math>, architecture family <math id=\"S2.p3.5.m5\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"A\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A</annotation></semantics></math>, training budget <math id=\"S2.p3.6.m6\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"B_{\\mathrm{train}}\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>train</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B_{\\mathrm{train}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and inference budget <math id=\"S2.p3.7.m7\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"B_{\\mathrm{infer}}\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>infer</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B_{\\mathrm{infer}}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>\n352\t<table id=\"S2.E2\" class=\"ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table\">\n353\t\n354\t<tbody><tr class=\"ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline\">\n355\t<td class=\"ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft\"></td>\n356\t<td class=\"ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center\"><math id=\"S2.E2.m1\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"\\mathcal{M}_{\\mathrm{emp}}(f;q,\\mathcal{D},c,G,A,B_{\\mathrm{train}},B_{\\mathrm{infer}})=\\big(\\mathcal{L}_{\\mathrm{val}},Q_{\\mathrm{gen}},C_{\\mathrm{train}},C_{\\mathrm{infer}}\\big).\" display=\"block\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class=\"ltx_font_mathcaligraphic\">ℳ</mi><mi>emp</mi></msub><mo lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>;</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class=\"ltx_font_mathcaligraphic\">𝒟</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>train</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>infer</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo maxsize=\"1.200em\" minsize=\"1.200em\">(</mo><msub><mi class=\"ltx_font_mathcaligraphic\">ℒ</mi><mi>val</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>gen</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>train</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>infer</mi></msub><mo maxsize=\"1.200em\" minsize=\"1.200em\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace=\"0em\">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathcal{M}_{\\mathrm{emp}}(f;q,\\mathcal{D},c,G,A,B_{\\mathrm{train}},B_{\\mathrm{infer}})=\\big(\\mathcal{L}_{\\mathrm{val}},Q_{\\mathrm{gen}},C_{\\mathrm{train}},C_{\\mathrm{infer}}\\big).</annotation></semantics></math></td>\n357\t<td class=\"ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright\"></td>\n358\t<td rowspan=\"1\" class=\"ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right\"><span class=\"ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right\">(2)</span></td>\n359\t</tr></tbody>\n360\t</table>\n361\t<p id=\"S2.p3.13\" class=\"ltx_p\">Here <math id=\"S2.p3.8.m1\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"\\mathcal{L}_{\\mathrm{val}}\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><msub><mi class=\"ltx_font_mathcaligraphic\">ℒ</mi><mi>val</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathcal{L}_{\\mathrm{val}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a within-objective validation loss, <math id=\"S2.p3.9.m2\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"Q_{\\mathrm{gen}}\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>gen</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q_{\\mathrm{gen}}</annotation></semantics></math> contains task and perceptual generation measures, and <math id=\"S2.p3.10.m3\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"C_{\\mathrm{train}}\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>train</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C_{\\mathrm{train}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id=\"S2.p3.11.m4\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"C_{\\mathrm{infer}}\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>infer</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C_{\\mathrm{infer}}</annotation></semantics></math> record realized resource use. Losses from token likelihood, denoising, and flow matching are not compared numerically across objectives. A broad modelability claim should show that a ranking persists across several suitable <math id=\"S2.p3.12.m5\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"G\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">G</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id=\"S2.p3.13.m6\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"A\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A</annotation></semantics></math>, rather than selecting one architecture that favors a latent.</p>\n362\t</div>\n363\t<div id=\"S2.p4\" class=\"ltx_para\">\n364\t<p id=\"S2.p4.1\" class=\"ltx_p\">Streaming also requires a profile. Encoder lookahead can be defined as</p>\n365\t<table id=\"S2.E3\" class=\"ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table\">\n366\t\n367\t<tbody><tr class=\"ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline\">\n368\t<td class=\"ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft\"></td>\n369\t<td class=\"ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center\"><math id=\"S2.E3.m1\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"\\tau_{\\mathrm{enc}}=\\inf\\{\\,\\Delta\\geq 0:z_{t}\\text{ depends only on }x_{\\leq t/r+\\Delta}\\,\\},\" display=\"block\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>enc</mi></msub><mo rspace=\"0.1389em\">=</mo><mrow><mo lspace=\"0.1389em\" movablelimits=\"false\" rspace=\"0em\">inf</mo><mrow><mo rspace=\"0.170em\" stretchy=\"false\">{</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace=\"0.278em\" rspace=\"0.278em\">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">​</mo><mtext> depends only on </mtext><mo lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi></mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau_{\\mathrm{enc}}=\\inf\\{\\,\\Delta\\geq 0:z_{t}\\text{ depends only on }x_{\\leq t/r+\\Delta}\\,\\},</annotation></semantics></math></td>\n370\t<td class=\"ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright\"></td>\n371\t<td rowspan=\"1\" class=\"ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right\"><span class=\"ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right\">(3)</span></td>\n372\t</tr></tbody>\n373\t</table>\n374\t<p id=\"S2.p4.2\" class=\"ltx_p\">but system latency additionally includes decoder lookahead, block duration, critical-path network evaluations, computation time, and time to first audio. We therefore use streaming compatibility as a representation objective and report end-to-end latency for the complete pair.</p>\n375\t</div>\n376\t<section id=\"S2.SS1\" class=\"ltx_subsection\">\n377\t<h3 class=\"ltx_title ltx_title_subsection\">\n378\t<span class=\"ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection\">2.1 </span>A Four-Objective Representation Design Space</h3>\n379\t\n380\t<div id=\"S2.SS1.p1\" class=\"ltx_para\">\n381\t<p id=\"S2.SS1.p1.1\" class=\"ltx_p\">These four quantities define the design space used in the rest of the paper:</p>\n382\t<ul id=\"S2.I1\" class=\"ltx_itemize\">\n383\t<li id=\"S2.I1.i1\" class=\"ltx_item\" style=\"list-style-type:none;\">\n384\t<span class=\"ltx_tag ltx_tag_item\">•</span> \n385\t<div id=\"S2.I1.i1.p1\" class=\"ltx_para\">\n386\t<p id=\"S2.I1.i1.p1.1\" class=\"ltx_p\"><span id=\"S2.I1.i1.p1.1.1\" class=\"ltx_text ltx_font_bold\">Representation burden</span>: frame rate and per-frame capacity.</p>\n387\t</div>\n388\t</li>\n389\t<li id=\"S2.I1.i2\" class=\"ltx_item\" style=\"list-style-type:none;\">\n390\t<span class=\"ltx_tag ltx_tag_item\">•</span> \n391\t<div id=\"S2.I1.i2.p1\" class=\"ltx_para\">\n392\t<p id=\"S2.I1.i2.p1.1\" class=\"ltx_p\"><span id=\"S2.I1.i2.p1.1.1\" class=\"ltx_text ltx_font_bold\">Distortion</span>: how much perceptual quality is lost.</p>\n393\t</div>\n394\t</li>\n395\t<li id=\"S2.I1.i3\" class=\"ltx_item\" style=\"list-style-type:none;\">\n396\t<span class=\"ltx_tag ltx_tag_item\">•</span> \n397\t<div id=\"S2.I1.i3.p1\" class=\"ltx_para\">\n398\t<p id=\"S2.I1.i3.p1.1\" class=\"ltx_p\"><span id=\"S2.I1.i3.p1.1.1\" class=\"ltx_text ltx_font_bold\">Empirical modelability</span>: the profile in Equation <a href=\"#S2.E2\" title=\"In 2 Two Decisions Behind Audio Generation ‣ A Unifying Perspective on Audio Generative Modeling: Latent Representations and Modeling Strategies\" class=\"ltx_ref\"><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_ref_tag\">2</span></a> under declared tasks and budgets.</p>\n399\t</div>\n400\t</li>\n401\t<li id=\"S2.I1.i4\" class=\"ltx_item\" style=\"list-style-type:none;\">\n402\t<span class=\"ltx_tag ltx_tag_item\">•</span> \n403\t<div id=\"S2.I1.i4.p1\" class=\"ltx_para\">\n404\t<p id=\"S2.I1.i4.p1.1\" class=\"ltx_p\"><span id=\"S2.I1.i4.p1.1.1\" class=\"ltx_text ltx_font_bold\">Streaming compatibility</span>: encoder causality and the latency induced when the representation is paired with a generator and decoder.</p>\n405\t</div>\n406\t</li>\n407\t</ul>\n408\t</div>\n409\t<figure id=\"S2.F1\" class=\"ltx_figure\"><img src=\"2608.08667v1/x1.png\" id=\"S2.F1.g1\" class=\"ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square\" width=\"331\" height=\"278\" alt=\"Refer to caption\">\n410\t<figcaption class=\"ltx_caption ltx_centering\"><span class=\"ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure\">Figure 1: </span>A schematic view of four objectives in audio representation design. The tetrahedral drawing is a mnemonic for interacting Pareto objectives, not a metric geometry: the axes have different units and are not barycentric coordinates.</figcaption>\n411\t</figure>\n412\t<div id=\"S2.SS1.p2\" class=\"ltx_para\">\n413\t<p id=\"S2.SS1.p2.1\" class=\"ltx_p\">Concrete design questions couple these objectives: compression versus reconstruction quality; frame rate versus per-frame modeling burden; reconstruction-optimal versus generator-readable latents; fixed-rate versus content-adaptive streaming; and offline quality versus causal generation. The four values do not define a common numerical coordinate system. The figure is a design mnemonic. Empirical results should instead be reported as a Pareto set. Discrete and continuous representations are implementation choices within this space, not competing vertices.</p>\n414\t</div>\n415\t<div id=\"S2.SS1.p3\" class=\"ltx_para\">\n416\t<p id=\"S2.SS1.p3.1\" class=\"ltx_p\">Compatibility with a pretrained text LLM is not a fifth intrinsic axis. It is modelability relative to a particular model family <math id=\"S2.SS1.p3.1.m1\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"A\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A</annotation></semantics></math>. Discrete tokens fit its softmax interface but can dominate context length and need not align with text semantics. LLM-Codec <cite class=\"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep\">[<a href=\"#bib.bib11\" title=\"\" class=\"ltx_ref\"><span class=\"ltx_text\" style=\"font-size:90%;\">11</span></a>]</cite> addresses this directly by deriving its codebook from a pretrained LLM’s token embeddings, so that audio tokens occupy the same vocabulary space the language model already uses. Continuous latents require projectors and a generative output head but may align more naturally with hidden states. The relevant comparison fixes the shared backbone and compute budget.</p>\n417\t</div>\n418\t</section>\n419\t<section id=\"S2.SS2\" class=\"ltx_subsection\">\n420\t<h3 class=\"ltx_title ltx_title_subsection\">\n421\t<span class=\"ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection\">2.2 </span>When Is a Representation Easier to Model?</h3>\n422\t\n423\t<div id=\"S2.SS2.p1\" class=\"ltx_para\">\n424\t<p id=\"S2.SS2.p1.1\" class=\"ltx_p\">What does it mean to say that a latent is “easy to model”? It cannot mean that the latent carries an intrinsic difficulty score. Modelability depends on the task, dataset, conditioning, modeling scheme, architecture family, and both training and inference budgets. The same RVQ stream may be difficult for a flat transformer but easier for a global-over-time, local-over-depth architecture; the same continuous latent may fail under deterministic regression but work under a conditional flow head. A narrow comparison should match every field in Equation <a href=\"#S2.E2\" title=\"In 2 Two Decisions Behind Audio Generation ‣ A Unifying Perspective on Audio Generative Modeling: Latent Representations and Modeling Strategies\" class=\"ltx_ref\"><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_ref_tag\">2</span></a>. A broader claim should evaluate several suitable model families and report the resulting Pareto sets.</p>\n425\t</div>\n426\t<div id=\"S2.SS2.p2\" class=\"ltx_para\">\n427\t<p id=\"S2.SS2.p2.1\" class=\"ltx_p\">In practice, four measurements expose different failure modes:</p>\n428\t<ol id=\"S2.I2\" class=\"ltx_enumerate\">\n429\t<li id=\"S2.I2.i1\" class=\"ltx_item\" style=\"list-style-type:none;\">\n430\t<span class=\"ltx_tag ltx_tag_item\">1.</span> \n431\t<div id=\"S2.I2.i1.p1\" class=\"ltx_para\">\n432\t<p id=\"S2.I2.i1.p1.1\" class=\"ltx_p\"><span id=\"S2.I2.i1.p1.1.1\" class=\"ltx_text ltx_font_bold\">Sequence burden</span>: frames or tokens per second, including the effective expansion caused by multiple codebooks.</p>\n433\t</div>\n434\t</li>\n435\t<li id=\"S2.I2.i2\" class=\"ltx_item\" style=\"list-style-type:none;\">\n436\t<span class=\"ltx_tag ltx_tag_item\">2.</span> \n437\t<div id=\"S2.I2.i2.p1\" class=\"ltx_para\">\n438\t<p id=\"S2.I2.i2.p1.1\" class=\"ltx_p\"><span id=\"S2.I2.i2.p1.1.1\" class=\"ltx_text ltx_font_bold\">Predictive burden</span>: held-out loss under the same objective, latent normalization, model family, and training budget.</p>\n439\t</div>\n440\t</li>\n441\t<li id=\"S2.I2.i3\" class=\"ltx_item\" style=\"list-style-type:none;\">\n442\t<span class=\"ltx_tag ltx_tag_item\">3.</span> \n443\t<div id=\"S2.I2.i3.p1\" class=\"ltx_para\">\n444\t<p id=\"S2.I2.i3.p1.1\" class=\"ltx_p\"><span id=\"S2.I2.i3.p1.1.1\" class=\"ltx_text ltx_font_bold\">Scaling burden</span>: quality as parameters, context length, training FLOPs, or sampling steps increase.</p>\n445\t</div>\n446\t</li>\n447\t<li id=\"S2.I2.i4\" class=\"ltx_item\" style=\"list-style-type:none;\">\n448\t<span class=\"ltx_tag ltx_tag_item\">4.</span> \n449\t<div id=\"S2.I2.i4.p1\" class=\"ltx_para\">\n450\t<p id=\"S2.I2.i4.p1.1\" class=\"ltx_p\"><span id=\"S2.I2.i4.p1.1.1\" class=\"ltx_text ltx_font_bold\">Task burden</span>: semantic stability, speaker and prosody retention, and perceptual quality in generated samples, not only codec reconstruction.</p>\n451\t</div>\n452\t</li>\n453\t</ol>\n454\t<p id=\"S2.SS2.p2.2\" class=\"ltx_p\">No single one is sufficient. Loss values from different objectives or latent coordinate systems are not directly comparable. Low token entropy can be manufactured by collapsing useful information. Good reconstruction can coexist with an irregular latent distribution. Low training loss can reflect an easy but semantically empty target. The meaningful comparison is downstream quality at matched reconstruction quality, latency, and compute.</p>\n455\t</div>\n456\t<div id=\"Thmprinciple1\" class=\"ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_principle\">\n457\t<h6 class=\"ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem\">\n458\t<span class=\"ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem\"><span id=\"Thmprinciple1.1.1.1\" class=\"ltx_text ltx_font_bold\">Principle 1</span></span><span id=\"Thmprinciple1.2.2\" class=\"ltx_text ltx_font_bold\"> </span>(Reconstruction does not determine modelability)<span id=\"Thmprinciple1.3.3\" class=\"ltx_text ltx_font_bold\">.</span>\n459\t</h6>\n460\t<div id=\"Thmprinciple1.p1\" class=\"ltx_para\">\n461\t<p id=\"Thmprinciple1.p1.1\" class=\"ltx_p\">A reconstruction-only codec solves</p>\n462\t<table id=\"S2.E4\" class=\"ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table\">\n463\t<tbody>\n464\t<tr id=\"S2.E4X\" class=\"ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline\">\n465\t<td class=\"ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft\"></td>\n466\t<td class=\"ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell\"><math id=\"S2.E4X.2.1.1.m1\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"\\displaystyle(\\theta_{\\mathrm{rec}},\\phi_{\\mathrm{rec}})\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>rec</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>rec</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\displaystyle(\\theta_{\\mathrm{rec}},\\phi_{\\mathrm{rec}})</annotation></semantics></math></td>\n467\t<td class=\"ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell\"><math id=\"S2.E4X.3.2.2.m1\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"\\displaystyle=\\arg\\min_{\\theta,\\phi}\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mrow><mi></mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace=\"0.167em\">⁡</mo><munder><mi>min</mi><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ϕ</mi></mrow></munder></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\displaystyle=\\arg\\min_{\\theta,\\phi}</annotation></semantics></math></td>\n468\t<td class=\"ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright\"></td>\n469\t<td rowspan=\"2\" class=\"ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right\"><span class=\"ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right\">(4)</span></td>\n470\t</tr>\n471\t<tr id=\"S2.E4Xa\" class=\"ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline\">\n472\t<td class=\"ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft\"></td>\n473\t<td class=\"ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell\"></td>\n474\t<td class=\"ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell\"><math id=\"S2.E4Xa.2.1.1.m1\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"\\displaystyle\\quad\\mathbb{E}_{x}\\!\\left[\\rho\\!\\left(x,g_{\\phi}(f_{\\theta}(x))\\right)\\right].\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mpadded style=\"width:0.347em;\" width=\"0.347em\"><mi>ρ</mi></mpadded><mo lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace=\"0em\">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\displaystyle\\quad\\mathbb{E}_{x}\\!\\left[\\rho\\!\\left(x,g_{\\phi}(f_{\\theta}(x))\\right)\\right].</annotation></semantics></math></td>\n475\t<td class=\"ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright\"></td>\n476\t</tr>\n477\t</tbody>\n478\t</table>\n479\t<p id=\"Thmprinciple1.p1.2\" class=\"ltx_p\">A representation designed together with its generator instead solves an objective of the form</p>\n480\t<table id=\"S2.E5\" class=\"ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table\">\n481\t\n482\t<tbody><tr class=\"ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline\">\n483\t<td class=\"ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft\"></td>\n484\t<td class=\"ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center\"><math id=\"S2.E5.m1\" class=\"ltx_math_unparsed\" alttext=\"(\\theta^{\\star},\\phi^{\\star},\\psi^{\\star})=\\arg\\min_{\\theta,\\phi,\\psi}\\mathbb{E}_{x,c}\\!\\left[\\rho\\!\\left(x,g_{\\phi}(f_{\\theta}(x))\\right)+\\lambda\\,\\mathcal{L}_{\\mathrm{gen}}\\!\\left(f_{\\theta}(x);p_{\\psi}(\\cdot\\mid c)\\right)\\right].\" display=\"block\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>ψ</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>arg</mi><munder><mi>min</mi><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ψ</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo>[</mo><mpadded style=\"width:0.347em;\" width=\"0.347em\"><mi>ρ</mi></mpadded><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>λ</mi><msub><mi class=\"ltx_font_mathcaligraphic\">ℒ</mi><mi>gen</mi></msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><mo>;</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>ψ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mo lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">⋅</mo><mo lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0.167em\">∣</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo lspace=\"0em\">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(\\theta^{\\star},\\phi^{\\star},\\psi^{\\star})=\\arg\\min_{\\theta,\\phi,\\psi}\\mathbb{E}_{x,c}\\!\\left[\\rho\\!\\left(x,g_{\\phi}(f_{\\theta}(x))\\right)+\\lambda\\,\\mathcal{L}_{\\mathrm{gen}}\\!\\left(f_{\\theta}(x);p_{\\psi}(\\cdot\\mid c)\\right)\\right].</annotation></semantics></math></td>\n485\t<td class=\"ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright\"></td>\n486\t<td rowspan=\"1\" class=\"ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right\"><span class=\"ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right\">(5)</span></td>\n487\t</tr></tbody>\n488\t</table>\n489\t<p id=\"Thmprinciple1.p1.3\" class=\"ltx_p\">The first objective only requires the latent to work with its decoder. The second also requires it to work with the downstream generator. Their optima need not coincide because reconstruction quality does not specify how the latent distribution is organized for a finite generator. The information bottleneck provides a related view: a useful representation should preserve task-relevant information and discard irrelevant variability <cite class=\"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep\">[<a href=\"#bib.bib12\" title=\"\" class=\"ltx_ref\"><span class=\"ltx_text\" style=\"font-size:90%;\">12</span></a>]</cite>.</p>\n490\t</div>\n491\t</div>\n492\t<div id=\"S2.SS2.p3\" class=\"ltx_para\">\n493\t<p id=\"S2.SS2.p3.1\" class=\"ltx_p\">DAC <cite class=\"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep\">[<a href=\"#bib.bib13\" title=\"\" class=\"ltx_ref\"><span class=\"ltx_text\" style=\"font-size:90%;\">13</span></a>]</cite> documents this gap concretely. Its objective combines adversarial and perceptual losses without a dominant sample-level reconstruction term. Its reconstructions can therefore score poorly on waveform-level fidelity measures such as SI-SNR but score strongly on perceptual measures such as ViSQOL <cite class=\"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep\">[<a href=\"#bib.bib14\" title=\"\" class=\"ltx_ref\"><span class=\"ltx_text\" style=\"font-size:90%;\">14</span></a>]</cite>. The decoder resynthesizes a perceptually equivalent waveform rather than one that matches the reference sample by sample. This is a concrete instance of Principle <a href=\"#Thmprinciple1\" title=\"Principle 1 (Reconstruction does not determine modelability). ‣ 2.2 When Is a Representation Easier to Model? ‣ 2 Two Decisions Behind Audio Generation ‣ A Unifying Perspective on Audio Generative Modeling: Latent Representations and Modeling Strategies\" class=\"ltx_ref\"><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_ref_tag\">1</span></a>: a distortion measure evaluated on samples and one evaluated by a listener or a downstream task need not agree, and a latent judged only by the former has not yet been judged by the criterion a downstream system actually needs.</p>\n494\t</div>\n495\t</section>\n496\t<section id=\"S2.SS3\" class=\"ltx_subsection\">\n497\t<h3 class=\"ltx_title ltx_title_subsection\">\n498\t<span class=\"ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection\">2.3 </span>How Should a Latent’s Distribution Be Modeled?</h3>\n499\t\n500\t<div id=\"S2.SS3.p1\" class=\"ltx_para\">\n501\t\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"bf23e4ba-ece0-4e48-8dc3-d9821a946456","uuid":"06a8b4d4-12bf-40e0-8726-286aa96b351c","timestamp":"2026-08-11T21:15:27.938Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/inspire/qb-ilm/project/video-generation/chenxie-25019/hyr/iHateTheWorld555.github.io/.claude-review/projects/-inspire-qb-ilm-project-video-generation-chenxie-25019-hyr-iHateTheWorld555-github-io/bf23e4ba-ece0-4e48-8dc3-d9821a946456/tool-results/bkyldysnf.txt","content":"<!DOCTYPE html><html lang=\"en\">\n<head>\n<meta http-equiv=\"content-type\" content=\"text/html; charset=UTF-8\">\n<title>A Unifying Perspective on Audio Generative Modeling: Latent Representations and Modeling Strategies</title>\n<!--Generated on Sun Aug  9 12:21:03 2026 by LaTeXML (version 0.8.8) http://dlmf.nist.gov/LaTeXML/.-->\n<meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1, shrink-to-fit=no\">\n<link rel=\"stylesheet\" href=\"/static/browse/0.3.4/css/arxiv-html-papers-20260131.css\" type=\"text/css\">\n<script src=\"/static/browse/0.3.4/js/arxiv-html-papers-20260131.js\"></script>\n<script>\n  // Restore the saved color scheme preference, or\n  // enact the browser preference if \"automatic\", \n  // without expecting DOM load to have completed.\n  //\n  // Also restore any saved readingmode and ToC display preferences.\n  function initializeReadingPreferences() {\n    let saved_theme = localStorage